kind: binding
semantic_diff: >-
  Re-entry instruments on screen_pin_and_progress and preregister_acceptance,
  declared per node: the investigator seat runs on the first entry into the
  screen and the implementer seat on the first preregistration; every re-entry
  whose registered inputs are byte-unchanged the lead settles mechanically with no
  seat (pin digest plus standing note; the four-slice check on the byte-unchanged
  registration), and any registered-value touch keeps the full value-level seat.
  Which instrument answers the node at each entry is runtime binding
  (pave-spec section 2.1), so this lands as kind binding; no node is added or
  removed, no outcome, edge, check, or evidence definition changes, and the counts
  stay 32 / 95 / 14. Same
  check, cheaper instrument, mechanical evidence still produced - rule 9's bound -
  so check strength is unchanged. Why it holds for runs in general: a priced seat
  re-run over byte-unchanged inputs buys no evidence, in any run.
envelope_check: unchanged
plan_evidence: verified
usage_evidence: field
changelog_entry: >-
  Revision 2 (binding): lead-mechanical re-entry instruments on the design screen
  and the acceptance preregistration where registered inputs are unchanged. The
  head digest now equals the field root's live graph. Field evidence: across the
  first run's 154 design-boundary traversals, seats were re-dispatched for
  re-checks whose answer was already on disk (the 2026-08-31 ceremony-cuts
  amendment).
